      "math_academy_id": "q-154082",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "The mass $m$ of a tablet PC measures $314.7\\,\\text{g}$ to the nearest tenth of a gram. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement.",
      "worked_solution": "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{g}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{g}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =314.7\\,\\text{g}-0.05\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =314.65\\,\\text{g} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =314.7\\,\\text{g}+0.05\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =314.75\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $m$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n314.65\\,\\text{g}\\le m<314.75\\,\\text{g}.\n$$",
